INT. FUNERAL HOME - KITCHEN - NIGHT

Foil-covered casseroles. Wilting lilies. A kettle starts to whistle.

BRONAGH QUILTY, 50s, black dress, cuts a cake. EMEKA QUILTY, 40s, her brother, loosens his tie. CORDELIA QUILTY, 30s, the youngest, sits on the counter with her shoes in her hand.

BRONAGH
Somebody get that.

EMEKA
I've got it.

CORDELIA
He's got it.

Emeka snatches the kettle off the burner. The whistle dies.

BRONAGH
Mind the cake.

CORDELIA
Nobody wants the cake.

EMEKA
I want the cake.

BRONAGH
Nobody wants your cake, Emeka.

EMEKA
Mam made that cake.

BRONAGH
Mam made that cake in nineteen ninety-four.

CORDELIA
That's why it's hard.

EMEKA
It's tradition.

BRONAGH
It's a doorstop.

Emeka sets the kettle down. Hard.

EMEKA
She's been in the ground four hours.

BRONAGH
I know.

EMEKA
You're slicing her cake like it's a bake sale.

BRONAGH
Somebody has to feed the neighbours.

CORDELIA
You didn't have to put on the apron.

BRONAGH
It's Mam's apron.

CORDELIA
That's what I mean.

Bronagh puts down the knife. Silence. A fridge hums. Emeka pulls out a chair. Sits. Puts his head in his hands.

EMEKA
(muffled)
I never said goodbye.

CORDELIA
You said it on Tuesday.

EMEKA
She was asleep.

BRONAGH
She heard you.

EMEKA
You don't know that.

BRONAGH
I know she squeezed my hand when I said your name.

He looks up. His eyes are red. Cordelia slides off the counter. Bronagh picks up the knife again, and cuts the cake, slowly, into three pieces.

She puts one in front of each of them.
